I have broken the implementation into four Specs-Driven Development (SDD) modules.

These prompts are designed to be executed sequentially to maintain a clean state and avoid context drift.

Module 1: Infrastructure & Environment Scaffolding
Goal: Initialize the project structure, Deno 2.x environment, and Podman configuration.

Prompt:
Act as a Senior DevOps Engineer. Initialize a project named markdone-universal optimized for Podman Desktop 1.26+ on macOS.

Project Structure:
Create the following directory hierarchy:

/services: For conversion logic (Pandoc wrappers and Python sidecar).

/static: For the Preact/HTM frontend.

/uploads and /outputs: Ephemeral storage.

Technical Specs:

Deno Config: Generate a deno.json using Deno 2.x standards. Include tasks for dev (with --watch and --allow-all) and start.

Podman Kube Manifest: Create podman-kube.yaml for Kube Play.

Port mapping: 7482:7482.

Resource Requests: CPU 50m, RAM 128Mi (Sleep Mode).

Resource Limits: CPU 4, RAM 2Gi (Burst Mode for M3 Pro optimization).

Volume Mount: Map /Users/<USER>/Documents/VaultVault to /app/outputs/obsidian.

Containerfile: Use denoland/deno:alpine as the base. Install pandoc, python3, py3-pip, and libmupdf via apk.

Setup Script: Create a setup.sh that checks for Podman and Deno installations.

Module 2: The Orchestrator (Backend API)
Goal: Build the Deno/Oak server that manages the dual-engine logic (Universal vs. High-Fidelity).

Prompt:
Act as a Backend Architect. Implement the Deno/Oak server in server.ts with the following logic:

API Endpoints:

GET /health: Returns system status and verifies if pandoc and python3 are in the PATH.

POST /api/convert: Handles multipart/form-data.

Core Logic:

Engine Selection: >    - If the target is "Vault MD," route the file to a Python sidecar (services/high_fidelity_pdf.py) using Deno.Command.

For all other conversions (DOCX, HTML, PDF), use the Pandoc CLI.

M3 Pro Concurrency: Implement a std/async/Semaphore with a limit of 6 concurrent processes to leverage performance cores without hitting the 2Gi RAM limit.

High-Fidelity Engine: Implement the legacy PDFExtractor logic using PyMuPDF (fitz) within the Python sidecar to preserve 30MB+ FSI deck extraction quality.

Cleanup: Implement a Deno.cron or a setInterval that purges files in /uploads older than 10 minutes.

Module 3: Frontend (Staging Queue & Carbon-Lite UI)
Goal: Create the zero-build SPA using Preact, HTM, and Pico CSS with an IBM Carbon aesthetic.

Prompt:
Act as a Frontend Engineer. Build a single-page application in /static/index.html and /static/app.js.

UI Requirements:

Styling: Use Pico CSS for the layout foundation. Apply an IBM Carbon v11 color palette:

Background: #F4F4F4.

Primary Buttons: #24A148 (Carbon Green).

Typography: Use IBM Plex Sans via Google Fonts.

Staging Queue System: >    - Implement a "Staging Area" where users can drag-and-drop multiple PDFs.

Files should be listed in a table with metadata (Name, Size, Source Type).

Users can remove individual files before hitting "Convert All."

Feature Toggles: Add a "High-Fidelity Vault Mode" toggle that enables YAML frontmatter and PyMuPDF-based extraction.

State Management: Use Preact signals to track upload progress and conversion status for the batch.

Module 4: Integration & "Gold Standard" Testing
Goal: Ensure the end-to-end flow from Podman deployment to Vault vault synchronization works.

Prompt:
Act as a Quality Assurance Engineer. Create a final integration and test suite.

Tasks:

Deployment Script: Create deploy.sh that builds the image, stops any existing markdone pod, and runs podman kube play podman-kube.yaml.

Validation Script: Create a Deno script (tests/integration.ts) that:

Uploads a test 30MB PDF.

Verifies the output MD file exists in the mapped Vault vault directory.

Checks for correct YAML frontmatter in the output.

Graceful Shutdown: Ensure server.ts catches SIGINT and SIGTERM to clean the /uploads directory and close any active Pandoc sub-processes.

Documentation: Generate a README.md including the "Burst Mode" vs. "Sleep Mode" resource specs for Podman.